  "k0_text": "Elias had spent forty years listening to the rhythm of the North Sea, a cadence of crashing waves and groaning timber that had long since become the only language he truly understood. On a Tuesday evening, when the fog rolled in thick enough to swallow the horizon, he found the bottle wedged between the rocks at the base of the cliff. It was not the usual debris of plastic or driftwood, but a heavy, green glass cylinder sealed with wax, containing a single, folded piece of parchment that felt impossibly fragile against his calloused fingers.\n\nHe carried it up the spiral stairs, the wind howling against the lantern room’s glass, and broke the seal with trembling hands. The ink was faded, the handwriting shaky and desperate, dated three years prior. It was from a sailor who had been lost in a storm, writing not to a family member, but to \"whoever finds this, please tell them I saw the stars before the end.\" Elias read the words twice, the silence of the lighthouse suddenly feeling less like solitude and more like a shared breath. He realized then that his isolation was not a punishment, but a vigil, a bridge between the living and the forgotten.\n\nThat night, Elias did not just tend the light; he",
